{0 CamlCast}

A first-person raycasting engine in OCaml on top of SDL2 ([tsdl]).

A {!Camlcast.World} is a graph of independently-authored {!Camlcast.Room}s, each
in its own coordinates, joined at named doorways that you both see and walk
through: an open one is a portal, drawn recursively into the opening, and a
{!Camlcast.Transform} carries the camera and the ray across. Rooms are built
from wall {e segments} at any angle, not cells of a grid; the floor is an
inclined {!Camlcast.Plane} and the ceiling — per room — is an inclined plane or
open {!Camlcast.Sky}; walls carry their own heights and {!Camlcast.Material}s,
can be see-through, and can be hung with decals; {!Camlcast.Image} sprites stand
in the world facing the player; and there is mouse look with pitch. Each frame is
drawn into a CPU pixel buffer: the floor, ceiling and sky are cast per pixel, the
opaque walls (with their decals) are painted over them, then the sprites, then
the see-through walls last so the room behind shows through. Surfaces take their
patterns from a procedural {!Camlcast.Texture} and their colour from their
material, dimmed by the world's {!Camlcast.Atmosphere}. The buffer is then
scaled to the window by the GPU.

{1 Two guides}

- {{!page-"making-a-game"} {b Making a game on CamlCast}} — from an empty
  directory to a game, one engine feature at a time, with the demo that isolates
  each one. Start here if you want to build something on the engine.
- {{!page-"building-the-engine"} {b Building the engine from scratch}} — how the
  picture is drawn, rebuilt by hand in eight phases with every derivation written
  out. Start here if you want to know how a raycaster works.

{1 Two libraries}

The engine holds no content: not one colour, pattern, picture or room. What it
has instead are the types those things are values of, so a game supplies its own
and two games can share an engine without sharing a look.

- {b [camlcast]} — the engine. Geometry, ray casting, rendering, SDL.
- {b [camlcast.demo]} — the demos and the art they are made of, run by
  [camlcast-demo]. One small world per engine feature, plus the five-room
  showcase that has all of them at once. It is content, and it lives outside the
  library it is content for; it stays here because between them the demos
  exercise every corner of the engine.

{1 Building a game on it}

{!Camlcast.Engine.run_state} runs a state of whatever type the game likes, and
asks six things of it: [update], given the frame's length, the movement asked
for over it and what the player is pressing and holding through it; [view],
which world and which player to draw this frame from; [overlay], anything drawn
over the finished picture; [pointing], whether the mouse is working a cursor
over something the game has drawn instead of looking around; [finished],
whether the run is over; and [escape], whether that key ends the run — off
unless asked for, because a game with screens in it wants it for closing them.
Everything else a game keeps — phases, doors, a journal, a random seed, what it
has already built — stays on the game's side of the line and the engine stays a
pure function of what it is handed. Time passes only while the window has focus.

It reports how the run ended, as an {!Camlcast.Engine.ending}: [Closed] for a
window that was shut, [Left] for a run that finished on its own terms. Only
something that opens one window after another needs the difference.

Input arrives as controls and not as actions — {!Camlcast.Input.pressed},
{!Camlcast.Input.released}, {!Camlcast.Input.val-down} and
{!Camlcast.Input.held_for} over a key or a mouse button. What "interact" or
"journal" mean is a game's own table from the one to the other.

{!Camlcast.Engine.enter} is that loop over a bare {!Camlcast.World} and the
player walking it, plus an optional [grow : World.t -> Player.t -> World.t],
which it calls whenever the player crosses into another room.
{!Camlcast.Engine.run} is [enter] with the ending thrown away, for a program with
only one world to show.

A game that builds its world as the player explores it uses [grow] to stay far
enough ahead that the edge is never in shot: {!Camlcast.Config.max_portal_depth}
doorways, which is exactly as deep as the renderer looks through them.
{!Camlcast_demo.Endless} does this in miniature.

{1 Where to start}

{!Camlcast.Ray} carries the ray-versus-segment derivation and why the distance
it reports is free of fish-eye. {!Camlcast.Plane} carries the one-line equation
that casts the sloped floor and ceiling per pixel. {!Camlcast.Viewport} ties
them to the screen: the projection, the eye height, the pitch shear, and — since
the window is resizable and can go fullscreen at any frame — the rules that
decide what a resize does. {!Camlcast.Renderer} puts it all together.
{!Camlcast.World} carries both the portal machinery and the three primitives a
world grows by.

{1 The engine}

Each depends only on the ones above it.

- {!Camlcast.Config} — every tunable constant, in one place
- {!Camlcast.Result_ext} — [let*] and a short-circuiting range loop, for
  chaining fallible SDL calls
- {!Camlcast.Vec} — immutable 2-D vectors, with the dot and cross products the
  geometry needs
- {!Camlcast.Transform} — rigid motions between room-local coordinate frames
- {!Camlcast.Plane} — an inclined floor/ceiling plane and its casting equation
- {!Camlcast.Color} — 8-bit RGB, shading and blending
- {!Camlcast.Surface} — decoding a PNG or JPEG into plain bytes: the one place
  pixel formats appear
- {!Camlcast.Asset} — where a file is, searched relative to the executable
  rather than to a source tree
- {!Camlcast.Texture} — colour surface patterns, generated or loaded, and
  the wrapping value noise the generated ones are built from
- {!Camlcast.Material} — what a surface is made of: its pattern
- {!Camlcast.Door} — a leaf hung in a doorway: open or closed, and what of
- {!Camlcast.Atmosphere} — the air a world is seen through: its fog, its haze,
  and where its light comes from
- {!Camlcast.Sky} — the open sky where a room has no roof
- {!Camlcast.Image} — full-colour images with alpha, for decals and sprites
- {!Camlcast.Paint} — clipped rectangles, lines and pictures over a finished
  frame
- {!Camlcast.Font} — a bitmap font on a fixed grid: layout, wrapping and
  drawing
- {!Camlcast.Room} — one room's walls, thresholds, surfaces, sprites and
  collision
- {!Camlcast.World} — named rooms, linked portals, the world's air and spawn,
  and how a world grows
- {!Camlcast.Ray} — ray-versus-segment intersection: every wall the ray
  crosses, farthest first
- {!Camlcast.Player} — the camera, as position + unit [dir] + unit [right],
  plus the pitch it looks up and down by
- {!Camlcast.Viewport} — window size to camera geometry; the eye height, the
  pitch shear and the resize rules
- {!Camlcast.Sight} — what the crosshair is on, traced through doorways
- {!Camlcast.Input} — SDL keyboard and mouse-look, as engine intent
- {!Camlcast.Framebuffer} — a CPU pixel buffer and the streaming texture it
  uploads through
- {!Camlcast.Renderer} — the software renderer: casts the planes, paints the
  walls, per pixel
- {!Camlcast.Engine} — window lifetime, fullscreen state, and the game loop

{1 The demos}

[camlcast-demo] with no argument lists them; [camlcast-demo <name>] runs one.
Each is a single file with the feature it demonstrates as the only thing in it.

- {!Camlcast_demo.Catalogue} — every demo there is, and what each one is for
- {!Camlcast_demo.Masonry} — materials: one pattern function, applied at several colours
- {!Camlcast_demo.Gallery} — decals on the walls, sprites standing in the room
- {!Camlcast_demo.Glass} — see-through walls and the translucent pass
- {!Camlcast_demo.Slopes} — inclined floors and roofs, and a seamless threshold
- {!Camlcast_demo.Daylight} — the open sky, and two rooms under different ones
- {!Camlcast_demo.Haze} — atmosphere: the fade into the distance and the light
- {!Camlcast_demo.Portals} — doorways: the same room, joined in two places
- {!Camlcast_demo.Changing} — replacing a room: a sign that moves, rebuilt
  every frame
- {!Camlcast_demo.Floating} — sprites off the floor, and frames chosen rather
  than made
- {!Camlcast_demo.Dust} — a chamber of falling dust: every mote moved every
  frame
- {!Camlcast_demo.Chalk} — marking a wall where the crosshair is, on the face
  you see
- {!Camlcast_demo.Doors} — doors that open and shut, on both sides of the link
  at once
- {!Camlcast_demo.Targets} — what the crosshair is on, through a doorway
- {!Camlcast_demo.Trail} — traversal traces, as a return route
- {!Camlcast_demo.Endless} — the grow hook: a corridor built as you walk it
- {!Camlcast_demo.Phases} — [run_state]: a phase, a clock and a light going out
- {!Camlcast_demo.Overlay} — drawing over the finished world
- {!Camlcast_demo.Controls} — press versus hold, and letting go of the cursor
- {!Camlcast_demo.Loading} — art read from files, beside the generated kind
- {!Camlcast_demo.Text} — a bitmap font: wrapping, measuring, clipping, colour
- {!Camlcast_demo.Level} — [showcase]: the five-room level, with all of the
  above at once
- {!Camlcast_demo.Patterns} — masonry, joinery, tile and glazing
- {!Camlcast_demo.Pictures} — two decals and two sprites
- {!Camlcast_demo.Surfaces} — the demos' materials, daylight sky and air
